<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The four RF technologies, matching the flags reader mode enables.

  Every tag actions can run on belongs here, and actions are keyed on the UID, which every tag has
  regardless of what it is made of. When this listed only NfcA a tag of any other family could be
  scanned in the app and assigned an action, then never fire it: the system had no reason to launch
  an app that had not claimed it. Nothing said so, and the setup flow completed exactly as it does
  for a tag that works.

  IsoDep is deliberately absent. It is ISO 14443-4 over A or B, which is what bank cards and transit
  passes speak; claiming it would put this app in the chooser every time one is near the phone, for
  the sake of tags nobody buys to automate. The A and B entries below still cover their RF layer.

  Each tech-list is a separate alternative: the platform matches a tag when any one list is fully
  satisfied, so these are four independent claims rather than one claim on all four at once.
-->
<resources xmlns:xliff="urn:oasis:names:tc:xliff:document:1.2">
    <tech-list>
        <tech>android.nfc.tech.MifareUltralight</tech>
    </tech-list>
    <tech-list>
        <tech>android.nfc.tech.NfcA</tech>
    </tech-list>
    <tech-list>
        <tech>android.nfc.tech.NfcB</tech>
    </tech-list>
    <tech-list>
        <tech>android.nfc.tech.NfcF</tech>
    </tech-list>
    <tech-list>
        <tech>android.nfc.tech.NfcV</tech>
    </tech-list>
</resources>
